# Rust architecture (GUI wallet)

All Rust for this app lives under `src-tauri/` as the `shekyl-wallet` crate.
It embeds `shekyl-engine-core` (and related path crates from shekyl-core)
directly — there is no Wallet2 / `shekyl-ffi` path.

Composition and file-size rules that apply to every module are in
`27-composition-decomposition.mdc`. This file is the **where things go**
map for Rust specifically.

## Layout

```text
src-tauri/src/
  lib.rs              // module tree + Tauri invoke_handler
  main.rs             // binary entry
  state.rs            // AppState, network enum
  engine_session.rs   // thin Engine lifecycle shell
  commands.rs         // thin Tauri adaptors (prefer not to grow)
  transfer_history.rs // send/receive history projection (Serialize DTO)
  staking_view.rs     // WI-RPC-1 staking read projection
  drain_balance.rs    // drainable-P read projection
  shard_visual.rs     // shard preview + list commands
  daemon_*.rs         // daemon process + JSON-RPC to shekyld
  gui_config.rs       // desktop config paths
  validate.rs         // input validation at the command edge
  wallet_name.rs      // wallet file naming
```

## Where things go

| Kind of code | Home |
|--------------|------|
| Open / create / close / refresh / transfer one-shot | `engine_session` (shell) |
| Core → wire projection + unit tests | dedicated module (`transfer_history`, `staking_view`, `drain_balance`, …) |
| Tauri `#[tauri::command]` | thin function in `commands` **or** in the feature module that owns the DTO (see `shard_visual`); register in `lib.rs` |
| Daemon HTTP JSON-RPC types | `daemon_rpc` |
| Sidecar process lifecycle | `daemon_manager` |
| Shared app handles | `state` |

## Rules

- **`EngineSession` stays a shell.** No multi-struct projection layers, no
  large `From` tables, no feature unit-test suites inline. Delegate to
  projection modules.
- **One serializable DTO per wire surface.** Session and command share it;
  do not add an identity copy in `commands.rs` (see rule 27).
- **Path dependencies** on shekyl-core crates are the normal integration
  surface. Prefer the same core APIs wallet-rpc uses so embedder behavior
  stays aligned.
- **`#![deny(unsafe_code)]` spirit:** no new `unsafe` in this crate without
  a documented FFI or OS boundary need (this crate should not need it).
- **Pin versions in `Cargo.lock`.** Audit new crate additions.
- **Fail closed at the session boundary** for seal / credential / open
  faults (rule 82) — project honest errors, never empty values that lie.

## Adding a new Engine-backed read

1. Call the core method under the correct lock / `block_in_place` policy
   (match wallet-rpc when one exists).
2. Put `From<CoreType> for WireType` + `Serialize` + unit tests in a
   **new or existing projection module**, not in `engine_session`.
3. Add a thin `#[tauri::command]` that open-checks and returns the wire type.
4. Add a TS type in `src/types/` and a panel under `src/components/` that
   owns fetch + load state.
5. Keep every touched file under the file-size ratchet
   (`scripts/ci/file_size_ratchet.conf`).
